# Accessible generated content

## Purpose

Generated content must be accessible by default. Write and structure content so
that it can be understood, navigated, and reused by people using different
devices, assistive technologies, or reading abilities.

Accessibility is not optional polish. It is part of the content quality expected
before saving or publishing a document.

## Writing principles

Use clear, direct language:

- write short sentences;
- use common words when possible;
- explain technical terms the first time they appear;
- avoid idioms, vague wording, and unnecessary jargon;
- keep one idea per paragraph.

Structure content with meaningful headings. Do not skip heading levels only for
visual effect. A clear hierarchy helps screen reader users and improves document
navigation.

## Links

Link text must describe the destination or action.

Good examples:

- `Read the accessibility guide`
- `Download the annual report`
- `Contact the support team`

Avoid unclear link text:

- `Click here`
- `Read more`
- `This page`
- raw URLs when a descriptive label is possible

When generating internal elasticMS links, keep the visible link text meaningful
and use the correct internal link format for the field.

## Images and media

Provide meaningful alternative text for informative images.

Good alternative text describes the useful information carried by the image, not
only its appearance.

Decorative images should have empty alternative text when the target field or
template supports it.

For audio or video content, provide a transcript or equivalent text summary when
possible.

## Tables and lists

Use lists for groups of related items.

Use tables only for tabular data, not for layout. When generating a table, keep
headers short and descriptive, and make sure each row and column relationship is
clear.

## Documents and files

When linking to a file, make the file type and purpose clear in the link text.

Example:

```markdown
[Download the annual report PDF](ems://asset:{sha1}?type=application/pdf&name=annual-report.pdf)
```

If the file is used in an indexed asset field and a summary is available, ask
the user before generating the `_content` summary used for search indexing.

## WYSIWYG HTML

When generating WYSIWYG content:

- use semantic HTML elements;
- keep headings in logical order;
- do not use empty headings or empty links;
- avoid using bold text as a replacement for headings;
- avoid instructions based only on color, shape, or position;
- provide descriptive text for embedded assets and media.

## Language

Use the document language consistently. If a field or content type expects a
specific language, generate content in that language.

If a link or title points to content in another language, make that clear when it
helps the reader.

## Before saving content

Check that:

- headings describe the sections they introduce;
- link text is understandable outside of its surrounding sentence;
- images and media have useful text alternatives when needed;
- file links mention the file purpose or type;
- generated text is clear and concise;
- content does not rely only on color, layout, or visual cues;
- no placeholder text remains.
